"The finite auxiliary is is missing.","D":"The arrangement is expressed with **is + preparing**; Tuesday gives it future reference."}}},{"id":"474cf442-4cc2-5bfb-ad6a-6473f0b22c99","topicSlug":"future-forms","level":"A2","prompt":"Keep the when-clause in the present simple: I will call when I ____ the evening meal.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"preparing"},{"id":"B","text":"to prepare"},{"id":"C","text":"prepare"},{"id":"D","text":"will prepare"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Locate **when**. The future marker belongs in the main clause, while this time clause uses present simple.","explanation":"The ordinary future time clause after **when** uses present simple: **I prepare**.","keywords":["future-time-clause"],"skill":"future-time-clause","contextKey":"future-forms-49","fingerprint":"6383d85be7617be3058d392fb67a6681578f868589056926fe9da346bea6ff5a","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Locate **when**. The future marker belongs in the main clause, while this time clause uses present simple.","The ordinary future time clause after **when** uses present simple: **I prepare**.","Choose **prepare**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"An -ing form alone is not a finite verb for I in this clause.","B":"An infinitive cannot serve as the finite verb after when I.","C":"The ordinary future time clause after **when** uses present simple: **I prepare**.","D":"This ordinary when time clause uses a present form, not will."}}},{"id":"964432e4-adc5-55be-aafb-d8c0e7ff63e0","topicSlug":"future-forms","level":"A2","prompt":"Make a promise with will: I ____ the rowing boat tomorrow.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"will repair"},{"id":"B","text":"will to repair"},{"id":"C","text":"will repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"will repaired"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"The instruction asks for a promise with **will**. Check the form immediately after the modal.","explanation":"A promise uses **will + repair**. The modal already marks the future meaning; keep the main verb in its base form.","keywords":["will-form"],"skill":"will-form","contextKey":"future-forms-50","fingerprint":"5a872281f7b5c6352608edaf12670c01951359a2c96255e65f8543dea6485020","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["The instruction asks for a promise with **will**. Check the form immediately after the modal.","A promise uses **will + repair**. The modal already marks the future meaning; keep the main verb in its base form.","Choose **will repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"A promise uses **will + repair**. The modal already marks the future meaning; keep the main verb in its base form.","B":"Will takes a bare infinitive, without to.","C":"An -ing form cannot follow will alone; a continuous form would need be.","D":"A past form cannot directly follow will."}}},{"id":"2b6c8a41-6ab1-52ac-acfa-59adab39d777","topicSlug":"future-forms","level":"A2","prompt":"Complete the prior plan using going to: We ____ the rowing boat next week.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"are going to repair"},{"id":"B","text":"is going to repair"},{"id":"C","text":"are going to repairing"},{"id":"D","text":"going to repair"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"Find the subject, then build **be going to + base verb**.","explanation":"With **we**, use **are going to + repair** for the stated prior plan.","keywords":["going-to-form"],"skill":"going-to-form","contextKey":"future-forms-50","fingerprint":"74bcaeb2149a6d1a97e4b6d6b44510c66a00e07556f9a13f6b2409af63e3c3e6","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Find the subject, then build **be going to + base verb**.","With **we**, use **are going to + repair** for the stated prior plan.","Choose **are going to repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"With **we**, use **are going to + repair** for the stated prior plan.","B":"Is does not agree with we.","C":"To in this intention pattern takes the base verb, not -ing.","D":"The finite verb are is missing."}}},{"id":"3e94143c-cd92-5518-a876-2797dd1cdcb8","topicSlug":"future-forms","level":"A2","prompt":"Use the present continuous for a booked arrangement: She ____ the rowing boat on Tuesday.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"is repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"are repairing"},{"id":"C","text":"is repair"},{"id":"D","text":"repairing"}],"answerId":"A","hint":"A future time can accompany a present continuous arrangement. Check subject + be + -ing.","explanation":"The arrangement is expressed with **is + repairing**; Tuesday gives it future reference.","keywords":["arrangement"],"skill":"arrangement","contextKey":"future-forms-50","fingerprint":"fdf3c150a99df670f70eea727b56362ac139944abd48e915b868054f21e76e9b","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["A future time can accompany a present continuous arrangement. Check subject + be + -ing.","The arrangement is expressed with **is + repairing**; Tuesday gives it future reference.","Choose **is repairing**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"The arrangement is expressed with **is + repairing**; Tuesday gives it future reference.","B":"Are does not agree with she.","C":"The present continuous needs the -ing form after is.","D":"The finite auxiliary is is missing."}}},{"id":"8100bcb1-6ce1-55b8-a045-8cf987d55728","topicSlug":"future-forms","level":"A2","prompt":"Keep the when-clause in the present simple: I will call when I ____ the rowing boat.","choices":[{"id":"A","text":"repairing"},{"id":"B","text":"to repair"},{"id":"C","text":"repair"},{"id":"D","text":"will repair"}],"answerId":"C","hint":"Locate **when**. The future marker belongs in the main clause, while this time clause uses present simple.","explanation":"The ordinary future time clause after **when** uses present simple: **I repair**.","keywords":["future-time-clause"],"skill":"future-time-clause","contextKey":"future-forms-50","fingerprint":"7a0492d7d488999b6771aec6ac1f390291c268374bcd0d9812a37a7da49ee302","source":"seed","provenance":{"kind":"original-guidance-aligned","referenceIds":["bc-future-forms"],"version":"core-2026-09-v3","reviewStatus":"automated-checked; educator-review-pending"},"teaching":{"steps":["Locate **when**. The future marker belongs in the main clause, while this time clause uses present simple.","The ordinary future time clause after **when** uses present simple: **I repair**.","Choose **repair**. Read the complete sentence again and check both its form and its intended meaning."],"choiceReasons":{"A":"An -ing form alone is not a finite verb for I in this clause.","B":"An infinitive cannot serve as the finite verb after when I.","C":"The ordinary future time clause after **when** uses present simple: **I repair**.","D":"This ordinary when time clause uses a present form, not will."}}}],"chapter":{"introduction":["Choose a future form by the speaker’s intention: a decision, a plan, an arrangement or a timetable.","The form tells you how to build the sentence; the context tells you why that form is useful. Read each section with both questions in mind. The examples make one contrast at a time, while the practice mixes the skills so that you learn to recognise the pattern yourself."],"rules":[{"title":"Decisions and promises","explanation":"Use **will + base verb** for a decision made while speaking, a promise or a prediction. Will does not change with the subject. A time word such as tomorrow does not force will: the speaker’s meaning matters more than the date. In conversation, I will often becomes I’ll.","pattern":"I **will help**. / She **will not help**.","commonMistakes":["Do not add -s after will: she will helps is not the standard form."],"examples":["Make a promise with will: I **will prepare** the visitor guide tomorrow.","Make a promise with will: I **will restore** the old portrait tomorrow."],"skill":"form"},{"title":"Prior intentions","explanation":"Use **be going to + base verb** for an intention formed before speaking. The form of be agrees with the subject. Going to also introduces a prediction based on present evidence. A dark sky can justify It is going to rain, even if no one planned the rain.","pattern":"We **are going to leave**.","commonMistakes":["Going to and will can overlap. These questions explicitly name the construction rather than treating both as universally incompatible."],"examples":["Complete the prior plan using going to: We **are going to prepare** the visitor guide next week.","Complete the prior plan using going to: We **are going to restore** the old portrait next week."],"skill":"meaning"},{"title":"Arranged future events","explanation":"The present continuous can describe a future arrangement, especially when another person, place or booking is involved. A future time expression separates this use from an action happening now. Present continuous is not a general replacement for will in predictions.","pattern":"We **are meeting** the guide tomorrow.","commonMistakes":["It is raining tomorrow is unusual as a simple weather prediction; use a suitable prediction form."],"examples":["Use the present continuous for a booked arrangement: She **is preparing** the visitor guide on Tuesday.","Use the present continuous for a booked arrangement: She **is restoring** the old portrait on Tuesday."],"skill":"contrast"},{"title":"Timetables and future time clauses","explanation":"Use present simple for published timetables. After when, as soon as, before and until in an ordinary future time clause, use a present form. The main clause can contain will. This rule concerns time clauses, not indirect questions about what will happen.","pattern":"I’ll call **when I arrive**.","commonMistakes":["I wonder when she will arrive is an indirect question, so will is possible there."],"examples":["Keep the when-clause in the present simple: I will call when I **prepare** the visitor guide.","Keep the when-clause in the present simple: I will call when I **restore** the old portrait."],"skill":"application"}],"recap":["I **will help**. / She **will not help**.","We **are going to leave**.","We **are meeting** the guide tomorrow.","I’ll call **when I arrive**."]},"stage":"3 · Time and experience","prerequisites":["past-continuous"]}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT(version,slug) DO NOTHING;
